{
  "dom_id": "step-q334561",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+(Δx)^{5}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.4,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.4$ to $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}.",
  "worked_solution": "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.4,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.4) & =5(0.4)^{4}+(0.4)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.0256+1\\cdot 0.010\\,24 \\\\  & =0.128+0.010\\,24 \\\\  & =0.138\\,24 \\\\  & ≈0.138.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.2) & =5(0.2)^{4}+(0.2)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.0016+1\\cdot 0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.008+0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.008\\,32 \\\\  & ≈0.008.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.4}{0.2}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={2}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.138\\,24$ to $0.008\\,32.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.138\\,24}{0.008\\,32}=16.615\\,384…≈16.615.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p} & ≈16.615\\,384… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(16.615\\,384…) \\\\ p & ≈4.054….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,e(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
